Third-party software and data
=============================

Nothing below is copied into this repository unless it is listed under
"Included". Everything else is fetched or installed on your machine by
setup.sh, or by you.

Included in this repository
---------------------------
* Nothing third-party is vendored. The package.json / yarn.lock files in
  tools/shot and port/mp only name npm packages (puppeteer-core, Apache-2.0;
  ws, MIT) that `yarn install` downloads.
* docs/blur-port-log/index.html loads mermaid (MIT) from a CDN at view time.
* port/work/interp/interp.c is a hand-written x86 interpreter with its own
  decoder (no third-party code). port/harness/hashes.c contains small MD5 /
  SHA-1 / SHA-256 routines written for this project.

Fetched or linked at build time (not redistributed here)
--------------------------------------------------------
* Unicorn Engine 2.x (GPL-2.0-only): boots the packed executable up to its
  entry point and serves as the reference CPU for the tests. The harness links
  against it, so a built `blurrt` is subject to the GPL-2.0 if you ever
  distribute it. (One more reason to keep the generated binary private.)
* Capstone 5 (BSD-3-Clause): x86 decoder used by the lifter (pip install).
* vkd3d 2.1, libvkd3d-shader only (LGPL-2.1-or-later): D3D9 shader bytecode to
  SPIR-V. Downloaded from https://dl.winehq.org/vkd3d/source/ and built into
  third_party/ (git-ignored). See third_party/README.md.
* SPIRV-Cross (Apache-2.0): SPIR-V to GLSL.
* FFmpeg libavformat / libavcodec / libavutil / libswresample (LGPL-2.1+ or
  GPL, depending on how your distribution configured it): decodes Bink 1
  movies.
* LLVM / Clang: compiler (Apache-2.0 with LLVM exceptions).
* puppeteer-core (Apache-2.0), ws (MIT): optional test tooling.
* Only for the optional WebGPU shader pack: naga-cli (MIT / Apache-2.0),
  SPIRV-Tools (Apache-2.0), brotli.

Not included and not available here
-----------------------------------
* Blur.exe and all game data (c) Activision / Bizarre Creations.
* msvcr90.dll 9.0.21022.8 (c) Microsoft. Supply it yourself (see README).
* Lifted game code, object files, the runtime binary, shaders.bin,
  blur.wgslpack, warm lists: all derived from the game. setup.sh recreates the
  ones it can on your machine.

Address lists
-------------
port/recomp/funcs.tsv, blocks.txt, learned*.txt, seed_ranges.txt,
oracle_funcs*.txt and overrides.txt are lists of code addresses (and sizes) in
one specific build of Blur.exe, produced by static analysis and by observing
runs. They contain no instruction bytes and no game assets. They only help the
lifter find function entries. setup.sh checks the SHA-256 of your Blur.exe
against the build they were made for.
